Codex approach gate: preflight reported `ok`, but the companion call returned an empty payload — an incomplete call, so no independent approaches came back. Noting it once and proceeding; the design below is mine alone.

---

## Design

### Architecture

One new file, `settings.js`, at the repo root next to `app.js` (that's where `index.html` resolves scripts from). It's an IIFE that picks an environment from `location.hostname`, looks up that environment's base URL, and publishes a frozen-in-practice `window.AppSettings`:

```js
// Environment-specific API configuration. Selection is by hostname so a deploy
// needs no code change; an unrecognized host is treated as production.
(function (global) {
  const ENVIRONMENTS = {
    local:      { apiBaseUrl: "http://localhost:3000" },
    staging:    { apiBaseUrl: "https://api-staging.example.com" },
    production: { apiBaseUrl: "https://api.example.com" },
  };

  const HOSTNAME_ENVIRONMENTS = {
    "localhost":           "local",
    "127.0.0.1":           "local",
    "staging.example.com": "staging",
  };

  const name = HOSTNAME_ENVIRONMENTS[global.location.hostname] || "production";
  const { apiBaseUrl } = ENVIRONMENTS[name];

  global.AppSettings = { environment: name, apiBaseUrl, loginUrl: `${apiBaseUrl}/login` };
})(window);
```

Two separate maps rather than one: hostnames are deployment facts that change often, environments are the stable set. Several hostnames can point at one environment (`localhost` and `127.0.0.1` already do).

**The hostnames are placeholders.** I don't know your real staging and local hosts, so the spec will record them as assumptions to confirm rather than as fact.

### Changes to existing files

- `index.html:13` — add `<script src="settings.js"></script>` **before** the `app.js` tag.
- `app.js:2` — delete `const API_ENDPOINT = ...`.
- `app.js:5-7` — `login()` currently only mentions the endpoint in a comment; nothing executes it. I'd have the stub read `AppSettings.loginUrl` and include it in the existing log line, so the wiring is real and the active environment is visible in the console. Otherwise nothing reads the module and a typo in it stays invisible until the API call is someday written.

### Data flow and failure modes

`index.html` → `settings.js` runs at parse time and sets `window.AppSettings` → `app.js` reads it inside `login()`, after load.

- **Unknown hostname** → production, per your answer. Worth naming the cost you accepted: a staging box whose host isn't in the map talks to the production API silently.
- **Load order** → if the `settings.js` tag is missing or placed after `app.js`, `AppSettings` is undefined and `login()` throws when submitted. This is the cost of the global-plus-script-tag wiring; an ES module import would have made it a load-time error instead of a runtime one.
- No network, storage, or auth behavior changes — the endpoint value still isn't sent anywhere, because `login()` remains a stub.

### Testing

The repo has no test runner, no linter, and no build — `package.json` has zero dependencies and no scripts. So there's no established pattern to follow, which makes this the moment to decide whether to add one (cheapest before more code exists), or to keep the repo dependency-free and verify by loading the page.
